    a: "Visualization, also called mental imagery, is the practice of mentally rehearsing a performance in vivid detail before it happens. Athletes picture the movement, the environment, and the feelings of executing a skill, which trains focus and confidence in the same way a physical rep trains the body. It is one of the core mental skills taught by sport psychology professionals."
  },
  {
    q: "How long should an athlete visualize each day?",
    a: "Short, focused sessions work best: around 5 to 10 minutes of vivid imagery is a strong starting point. Consistency matters more than duration. Research on mental imagery suggests that regular brief practice paired with physical training produces better results than occasional long sessions."
  },
  {
    q: "Is internal or external visualization better?",
    a: "Neither is universally better; they serve different purposes. Internal imagery (seeing through your own eyes) feels closest to real performance and suits execution and confidence. External imagery (watching yourself as if on video) helps when analyzing form and correcting technique. Many athletes use both within a single session."
  },
  {
    q: "Can visualization replace physical practice?",
    a: "No. Visualization is a supplement to physical training, not a replacement. The best results come from pairing mental reps with real reps, so the mind and body learn the same movement together. It is also not a treatment for injuries; during recovery it can be used as mental rehearsal alongside proper medical care."
  },
  {
    q: "Why does my visualization feel vague or hard to control?",
    a: "Vague imagery usually means the scene is not specific enough. Pick one exact skill, define the setting in detail, and add sensory cues such as sound, touch, and movement speed. Like any skill, imagery control improves with regular short practice. Keep sessions brief, stay focused on one rep at a time, and expect control to build over weeks, not days."
  }
];
