  <p>Visualization, also called mental imagery, is the deliberate mental rehearsal of a sport performance before it happens. An athlete closes their eyes and runs through a skill or a competition sequence in vivid detail: the sights, the sounds, the physical sensations, and even the emotions of executing it well. It is not daydreaming or positive thinking; it is structured practice that happens in the mind.</p>

  <p>Athletes use visualization in many ways. A basketball player might rehearse free throws before a game. A sprinter might walk through the start sequence from the blocks. A golfer might picture the line of a putt before stepping up to it. Research on mental imagery suggests that this kind of rehearsal supports focus and confidence, especially when it is paired with real physical practice rather than used on its own.</p>

  <p>This guide explains what visualization is, how the two main perspectives differ, a step-by-step way to practice, the best times to use it, how to make it a lasting habit, and the mistakes that make it a waste of time. It is a trainable skill, not a talent, and like any skill it responds to clear method and regular repetition.</p>

  <h2 id="what-is-visualization">What visualization (mental imagery) is</h2>

  <p>At its core, visualization is a simulation of performance built from memory and imagination. The athlete creates a mental movie of a skill, play, or entire competition, and runs it with as much sensory detail as possible. The more the mental image matches the real experience, the more useful the rehearsal becomes.</p>

  <p>What gets rehearsed varies by sport, but the structure is the same. Movement patterns are the most common target: the feel of a swing, the rhythm of a stride, the timing of a release. Athletes also rehearse decision points, such as reading a defender or choosing a line, and emotional responses, such as staying calm after a mistake. A swimmer might picture the turn, feel the push off the wall, and hear the water. A tennis player might feel the toss, see the ball, and hear the contact.</p>

  <p>Why does mental rehearsal help at all? Three reasons show up again and again. First, it trains attention: rehearsing a skill forces the mind to dwell on the cues that matter and ignore the rest. Second, it builds familiarity: competition feels less strange when the athlete has already experienced it mentally many times. Third, it supports confidence: a bank of mental reps of successful execution gives the athlete something to draw on under pressure. Research on mental imagery generally supports these benefits when imagery is specific, regular, and combined with physical training.</p>

  <p>Visualization is one of the core skills in a sport psychology toolkit, alongside goal setting, self-talk, and focus routines. You can read more about how these skills fit together in our overview of <a href="/mental-skills/">mental skills</a>, and about the professionals who teach them in our guide to <a href="/careers/">careers in sport psychology</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="perspectives">Internal vs external perspective: two ways to picture performance</h2>

  <p>There are two main ways to picture a performance. In internal imagery, the athlete sees through their own eyes, as if they were performing right now. They feel their body move, see the field or court from eye level, and hear the sounds around them. This perspective feels closest to the real thing, and it is the natural choice for rehearsing how an action feels under pressure.</p>

  <p>In external imagery, the athlete watches themselves from outside, as if seeing a video replay. They can observe posture, spacing, and timing from a coach's point of view. This perspective is useful for analyzing form, because it lets the athlete see the whole movement instead of living inside it.</p>

  <p>Neither perspective is better in all cases. Internal imagery suits execution and confidence: feeling the perfect rep as if it were happening now. External imagery suits learning and correction: watching the ideal technique and comparing it with your own. Many experienced athletes switch between the two inside a single session, using external imagery to check form and internal imagery to lock in the feeling of executing it.</p>

